# Layout: blocks marked by indentation

A language whose blocks are marked by indentation needs its lexer to say where a block opens, where
it closes and where a statement ends. Those are questions about the lines around a line, not about
its characters, and every such language answers them by one rule, the offside rule: a line indented
further than its block opens a block in it, a line indented less closes each block it leaves, and
a line's end ends its statement. What varies from one language to the next is small: how far a
block is indented, which lines carry on the line above, which brackets make line ends white space,
and which tokens a line may hold and still count as blank.

So the lexer is written with the string combinators, and does not keep the rule. It marks where
each line begins, with a token holding the line's `Margin`, its indentation as `margin` reads it,
and emits the line's tokens after it. A `Layout` then reads that stream and gives it back with the
marks taken out and the language's indent, dedent and newline tokens put in, and a list of the
lines whose indentation is wrong. The token type is the language's own: the layout is told how to
recognise a mark and how to make the three tokens it adds.

    module dev.avinlang.parsing

## Reading a line's margin

A line's margin is the spaces and tabs it begins with. Its width counts them, a tab as one column,
since a tab is an error a layout reports rather than a width it guesses at; `start` is where the
line begins and `text` where what follows the margin begins.

    // The indentation a line begins with: how many spaces and tabs, whether a tab is among them,
    // where the line begins and where its text begins.
    data Margin(width: int, tabbed: bool, start: Position, text: Position)

`margin` reads one, at the start of a line. It skips no white space, which would read the margin
itself, and it reads nothing at all where the line is not indented, so it always succeeds.

    // The spaces and tabs at the start of a line, read as its margin.
    lazy val margin: Parser[Margin] = Parser(input ->
      val start = input.offset
      var tabbed = false
      while !input.atEnd() && isMarginByte(input, input.offset)
        if char(int(input.source.byteAt(input.offset))) == '\t' then tabbed = true
        input.offset += 1
      Some(Margin(input.offset - start, tabbed, input.position(start), input.pos())))

    // Whether the byte at `at` is a space or a tab.
    private isMarginByte(input: Input, at: int) -> bool = char(int(input.source.byteAt(at))) match
      ' ' | '\t' -> true
      _ -> false

`lineBreak` reads a line's end, `\r\n` or `\n`, after white space, so the spaces after a line's last
token go with it. A lexer of lines is then lines separated by line breaks, each a margin and the
tokens after it:

```avin
lazy val line: Parser[List[Tok]] = (margin ^^ (m -> Mark(m))) ~ rep(not(lineBreak) ~> item) ^^ (match (m, ts) -> m :: ts)
lazy val lines: Parser[List[List[Tok]]] = repsep(line, lineBreak)
```

where the grammar's white space is spaces and tabs, and comments if the language drops them, but
never a line break.

    // The end of a line, `\r\n` or `\n`, after white space.
    lazy val lineBreak: Parser[string] = (literal("\r\n") | literal("\n")).withFailureMessage("end of line")

## The rule

The layout keeps the indentation of each open block, the outermost at width 0, and reads the
tokens in order. At a line's first token, what the line means is decided:

- A line holding nothing but white space, or only tokens the language calls **trivia** (comments it
  keeps), is no line at all: it opens nothing, closes nothing and ends nothing. Its trivia stay in
  the stream where they stand.
- A line beginning with a token the language says **continues** the line above, and indented
  further than the block it is in, carries on the line above: no newline comes between them.
- Any other line ends the one above with a **newline**, placed where that line's last token ended,
  and then lines up: indented further than its block, it opens a block with an **indent**; indented
  less, it closes each block it leaves with a **dedent**, both placed at the line's margin.

Inside a bracket the language **opens** and does not yet **close**, line ends are white space: the
marks there are dropped and decide nothing. At the end, the last line ends with a newline, and every
block still open closes with a dedent at the end of the text.

A block is indented by a fixed **step**, or by any amount deeper when the step is 0. Three things
can be wrong, and each is reported at the start of its line while the layout carries on, so one
text reports every mistake:

| Error | When | What the layout does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TabIndent(at)` | the margin holds a tab | reads the line as one step into its block where it is indented further, and in its block otherwise, so the one mistake is reported once |
| `UnevenIndent(at, outer, step)` | a line opens a block indented other than `step` past the one it is in | opens the block where the line is |
| `Misaligned(at, inner, outer)` | a line indented less than its block lines up with no block it is in, falling between `inner` and the block outside it, `outer` | keeps the line in the inner block, which is indented as the line is from then on |

    // A line whose indentation is wrong: each is at the start of its line, `at`.
    data LayoutError
      // The margin holds a tab.
      TabIndent(at: Margin)
      // A line opens a block indented other than `step` past the block it is in, at `outer`.
      UnevenIndent(at: Margin, outer: int, step: int)
      // A line indented less than its block, at `inner`, and more than the block outside it, at
      // `outer`.
      Misaligned(at: Margin, inner: int, outer: int)

    impl LayoutError
      // The margin of the line in error.
      margin() -> Margin = self match
        TabIndent(m) -> m
        UnevenIndent(m, _, _) -> m
        Misaligned(m, _, _) -> m

      // Where the error is: the start of its line.
      pos() -> Position = margin().start

      // What is wrong, in words.
      message() -> string = self match
        TabIndent(_) -> "indentation uses a tab"
        UnevenIndent(
          m,
          outer,
          step
        ) -> s"this line is indented ${m.width - outer} past its block, and a block is indented $step"
        Misaligned(
          m,
          inner,
          outer
        ) -> s"this line's indentation, ${m.width}, lines up with no enclosing block: its block is indented $inner and the one outside it $outer"

    // An error shows as its line and column and its message.
    impl Display for LayoutError
      display(out: Writer, spec: FormatSpec) = out.write(s"${pos()}: ${message()}")

What a layout answers is the tokens with the marks taken out and the layout tokens put in, and the
errors in the order of their lines.

    // The tokens a layout made, and the indentation errors it found.
    class LaidOut[E](val tokens: Vector[E], val errors: List[LayoutError])

## A language's layout

A language says what varies by conforming to `Layout[E]`, for its token type `E`. Five things it
must say, since only it knows its tokens: which token marks a line and with what margin, how to make
an indent, a dedent and a newline, and where a token ends. The rest have defaults: any deeper
indentation opens a block, no line continues another, no bracket holds line ends, and every token
counts.

```avin
object Lines: Layout[Tok]
  override lineStart(t: Tok) -> Option[Margin] = t match
    Mark(m) -> Some(m)
    _ -> None
  override indentToken(at: Margin) -> Tok = Indent(at.text)
  override dedentToken(at: Margin) -> Tok = Dedent(at.text)
  override newlineToken(at: Position) -> Tok = Newline(at)
  override tokenEnd(t: Tok) -> Position = t.end
  override step() -> int = 2
  override continues(t: Tok) -> bool = t is Dot(_)
  override opens(t: Tok) -> bool = t is Open(_)
  override closes(t: Tok) -> bool = t is Close(_)
```

The dedents at the end of the text are given a margin of width 0 whose start and text are both
`end`.

    // How a language's lines make blocks: the tokens that mark a line and the tokens a layout adds,
    // and what decides whether a line opens, closes, continues or ends one.
    trait Layout[E]
      // The margin of the line `t` marks the start of, or `None` when `t` is not a mark.
      lineStart(t: E) -> Option[Margin]

      // The token opening a block at the line whose margin is `at`.
      indentToken(at: Margin) -> E

      // The token closing a block, at the line whose margin is `at`.
      dedentToken(at: Margin) -> E

      // The token ending a line whose last token ends at `at`.
      newlineToken(at: Position) -> E

      // Where `t` ends in the text.
      tokenEnd(t: E) -> Position

      // How far a block is indented past the one it is in, or 0 for any amount.
      step() -> int = 0

      // Whether a line beginning with `t`, indented further than its block, carries on the line
      // above.
      continues(t: E) -> bool = false

      // Whether `t` opens a bracket, inside which line ends are white space.
      opens(t: E) -> bool = false

      // Whether `t` closes the bracket open.
      closes(t: E) -> bool = false

      // Whether `t` is trivia: kept in place, but leaving a line that holds only it blank.
      trivia(t: E) -> bool = false

      // `items` with their marks replaced by indents, dedents and newlines, the text ending at `end`.
      layout(items: Vector[E], end: Position) -> LaidOut[E]
        val run = Offside(self, end)
        for t in items
          run.take(t)
        run.finish()

## Applying the rule

A run of the rule keeps the open blocks' widths, innermost first, and the bracket depth. A line's
mark is held until its first token comes, since only that token says whether the line continues
the one above; trivia that come before it are held too, so they stand after the newline that ends
the line above and before the line's indents or dedents.

    // One application of a layout to a stream of tokens.
    private class Offside[E](val rules: Layout[E], val end: Position)
      // The tokens made so far.
      val out = Buffer[E]()

      // The errors found so far.
      val errors = Buffer[LayoutError]()

      // Trivia held until the line they stand before is laid out.
      val held = Buffer[E]()

      // The widths of the open blocks, innermost first; the outermost is 0.
      var levels = List(0)

      // How many brackets are open.
      var depth = 0

      // The margin of the line whose first token has not come yet.
      var pending: Option[Margin] = None

      // Whether a line with a token in it has been read.
      var started = false

      // Whether the line being read has a token in it that is not trivia.
      var filled = false

      // Where the last token that is not trivia ended.
      var lastEnd = Position(0, 1, 1)

      // Reads the next token.
      take(t: E) = rules.lineStart(t) match
        Some(m) ->
          if depth == 0
            pending = Some(m)
            filled = false
        None ->
          if rules.trivia(t)
            if filled then out += t else held += t
          else
            pending match
              Some(m) -> startLine(m, t)
              None -> flushHeld()
            pending = None
            started = true
            filled = true
            out += t
            lastEnd = rules.tokenEnd(t)
            if rules.opens(t)
              depth += 1
            else if rules.closes(t) && depth > 0
              depth -= 1

      // Decides what the line with margin `m` and first token `t` means, and lays it out.
      private startLine(m: Margin, t: E)
        val top = levels.head
        var width = m.width
        if m.tabbed
          errors += TabIndent(m)
          width = if width <= top then top else if rules.step() > 0 then top + rules.step() else width
        if started && width > top && rules.continues(t)
          flushHeld()
          return
        if started then out += rules.newlineToken(lastEnd)
        flushHeld()
        indentTo(width, m)

      // Opens or closes blocks so that a line indented `width` is in its own.
      private indentTo(width: int, m: Margin)
        val top = levels.head
        if width > top
          if rules.step() > 0 && width != top + rules.step() then errors += UnevenIndent(m, top, rules.step())
          levels = width :: levels
          out += rules.indentToken(m)
        else
          while width < levels.head
            val outer = levels.tail.head
            if outer < width
              errors += Misaligned(m, levels.head, outer)
              levels = width :: levels.tail
              return
            levels = levels.tail
            out += rules.dedentToken(m)

      // Moves the held trivia to the output.
      private flushHeld()
        for t in held
          out += t
        held.clear()

      // Ends the last line, closes every block still open, and answers what was made.
      finish() -> LaidOut[E]
        if started then out += rules.newlineToken(lastEnd)
        flushHeld()
        val at = Margin(0, false, end, end)
        while !levels.tail.isEmpty
          levels = levels.tail
          out += rules.dedentToken(at)
        LaidOut(out.toVector, errors.toList)
